import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var o={newtonsSecondLaw:{theory:{concept:"[[Newton's second law: the acceleration of a body is directly proportional to the net force acting on it, inversely proportional to its mass, and points in the direction of the net force: a = F_net / m.]] The net force is the vector sum of all the forces acting on the body. In a more general form, the net force equals the rate of change of momentum: F_net = \u0394p / \u0394t."},section:{law:{title:"The second law",text:"[[Double the net force and the acceleration doubles; double the mass and it halves.]] The law was published by Isaac Newton in 1687 and is still the basis of every calculation of motion, from cars to spacecraft."},netForce:{title:"Only the net force counts",text:"A body usually feels several forces at once. [[What changes its motion is their sum, the net force.]] On the box, the pull F acts forwards and friction f backwards, so F_net = F \u2212 f. Weight and the push of the track (the normal force) also act, but they are equal and opposite, so they cancel and the box does not move up or down. If all forces cancel, F_net = 0 and the body keeps its speed \u2014 that is Newton's first law."},mass:{title:"Mass is inertia",text:"Mass measures how hard it is to change a body's motion. A full shopping trolley is harder to start and to stop than an empty one, even on a perfectly smooth floor. That resistance is called inertia, and mass is its measure. Mass is not the same as weight: on the Moon a box weighs six times less, but it is just as hard to get moving sideways."},friction:{title:"Friction",text:"Friction acts where the box touches the track and opposes sliding. While the box slides, f = \u03BC \xB7 N, where N is the normal force (here N = m \xB7 g) and \u03BC is a number that depends on the two surfaces: about 0.05 for ice, 0.3 for wood on wood, 0.7 for rubber on concrete. At rest, friction is only as strong as needed to hold the box: push with 5 N and it pushes back with 5 N. Only when the pull is bigger than \u03BC \xB7 N does the box start to move."},newton:{title:"The newton",text:"The unit of force is named after Newton. One newton is the force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m/s\xB2: 1 N = 1 kg \xB7 m/s\xB2. An apple weighs about 1 N; a 50 kg student weighs about 490 N."},graphs:{title:"What the graphs show",text:"With a constant net force the acceleration is constant, so the speed grows by the same amount every second: the speed\u2013time graph is a straight line whose slope is a. The distance grows faster and faster: x = v\u2080\xB7t + \xBD\xB7a\xB7t\xB2, a parabola. The dots on the track mark the box every half second; their gaps grow as 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 when the box starts from rest."},everyday:{title:"In everyday life",text:"A sports car and a lorry with the same engine force: the light car accelerates much faster. A footballer kicks a light ball far, but the same kick barely moves a heavy medicine ball. In a crash a seat belt spreads the stopping over a longer time, so the force on your body is smaller. Engineers design rockets, lifts and brakes with F = m \xB7 a."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A 2 kg box is pulled with 10 N on smooth ice. What is its acceleration? (5 m/s\xB2.) The mass doubles. (2.5 m/s\xB2.) A 20 N pull acts against 8 N of friction on a 4 kg box. (F_net = 12 N, a = 3 m/s\xB2.) A box moves at constant speed. What is the net force? (Zero.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{law:"Newton's second law: acceleration from net force and mass",net:"The net force: the pull minus friction",newton:"The newton: the force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m/s\xB2",friction:"Sliding friction (N: normal force, here equal to the weight)",speed:"Speed after time t with constant acceleration",position:"Distance after time t with constant acceleration",momentum:"The second law with momentum: force is the rate of change of momentum"},variable:{force:"Pulling force",friction:"Friction force",net:"Net force",mass:"Mass",acceleration:"Acceleration",mu:"Friction coefficient",gravity:"Gravitational field strength"},assumption:{point:"The box is treated as a point on a level track; air resistance is ignored.",friction:"One friction coefficient is used both at rest and while sliding (real static friction is usually a little larger).",force:"The pulling force is constant and horizontal.",track:"Not to scale: the box and the arrows are drawn larger; arrows of forces share one scale, so their lengths can be compared. The run ends at the end of the 20 m track or after 15 s."},param:{force:"Pulling force",mass:"Mass of the box",friction:"Friction coefficient \u03BC",initialSpeed:"Starting speed",gravity:"Gravity",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{force:"A constant horizontal pull to the right.",mass:"A heavier box has more inertia.",friction:"0 is a perfectly smooth track. Ice is about 0.05, wood 0.3, rubber 0.7.",initialSpeed:"Give the box a push before the run starts.",gravity:"Gravity changes the weight, and with it the friction \u2014 but not the mass."},preset:{gentle:"Gentle",medium:"Medium",strong:"Strong",light:"Light",normal:"Normal",heavy:"Heavy",noFriction:"Smooth",ice:"Ice",wood:"Wood",rubber:"Rubber"},measure:{netForce:"Net force",position:"Distance travelled",frictionForce:"Friction force",endSpeed:"Speed at the finish",weight:"Weight",normalForce:"Normal force",momentum:"Momentum"},view:{finish:"Finish",law:"a = F_net / m = {net} / {mass} = {acceleration}",held:"Friction holds the box",velocityOption:"Velocity",verticalOption:"Weight and normal force"},graph:{speed:"Speed against time",position:"Distance against time",acceleration:"Acceleration against time"},challenge:{twoPerSecond:{title:"2 m/s\xB2 with a heavy box",description:"Make a box of at least 3 kg accelerate at exactly 2 m/s\xB2.",hint:"F = m \xB7 a: a 3 kg box needs 6 N, a 5 kg box 10 N."},heavyBox:{title:"Moving the heavy one",description:"Give a box of 10 kg or more an acceleration of exactly 1 m/s\xB2.",hint:"Each kilogram needs 1 N for 1 m/s\xB2."},fastFinish:{title:"Fast at the finish",description:"Starting from rest, cross the finish line at 12 m/s or faster.",hint:"v\xB2 = 2 \xB7 a \xB7 L, so on 20 m you need a \u2265 3.6 m/s\xB2."},frictionHolds:{title:"It will not budge",description:"Pull with at least 20 N and still have the box stay where it is.",hint:"Friction can hold up to \u03BC \xB7 m \xB7 g. Choose rubber and a heavy box."},steadySpeed:{title:"Steady speed",description:"Give the box a starting speed and make it keep exactly that speed, on a track with friction.",hint:"Constant speed means zero net force: pull exactly as hard as friction, F = \u03BC \xB7 m \xB7 g."},moonPush:{title:"Easier on the Moon",description:"On the Moon, move a box from rest with a pull that would not move it on the Earth.",hint:"On the Moon the box weighs less, so friction \u03BC \xB7 m \xB7 g is smaller \u2014 but its mass is the same."}}}},a={sim:o};export{a as default,o as sim};
